{"query":"site:github.com/security/advisories \"XSS\" markdown rendering","data":"A stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability was... · CVE-2026-77912 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/ghsa-6293-4rx2-2p57)\n【turn804800search0】 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last week; A stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability was identified in GitHub Enterprise Server that allowed an authenticated attacker to inject arbitrary HTML attributes into rendered Markdown because the Markdown rendering pipeline rewrote quote characters in already-sanitized HTML without re-sanitizing the result. Crafted Markdown could abuse same-origin JavaScript gadgets to bypass Content Security Policy and gain control of the page DOM when viewed by another user.\n\n## A stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability was...\n\nHigh severity Unreviewed Published Sep 22, 2026 to the GitHub Advisory Database • Updated Oct 2, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nNo package listed— Suggest a package\n\n## Affected versions\n\nUnknown\n\n## Patched versions\n\nUnknown\n\n## Description\n\nA stored cross-site scripting (XSS) vulnerability was identified in GitHub Enterprise Server that allowed an authenticated attacker to inject arbitrary HTML attributes into rendered Markdown because the Markdown rendering pipeline rewrote quote characters in already-sanitized HTML without re-sanitizing the result. Crafted Markdown could abuse same-origin JavaScript gadgets to bypass Content Security Policy and gain control of the page DOM when viewed by another user. Successful exploitation could allow an attacker to read content visible to the victim, extract embedded CSRF tokens, perform state-changing actions as the victim, and exfiltrate data through same-origin writes. The payload could also propagate to repositories and organizations where the victim had write access. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[A05 Injection] XSS via unsanitized curriculum markdown rendering · Advisory · freeCodeCamp/freeCodeCamp · GitHub (https://github.com/freeCodeCamp/freeCodeCamp/security/advisories/GHSA-c7cm-vx87-g6xj)\n【turn804800search1】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # [A05 Injection] XSS via unsanitized curriculum markdown rendering ... `add-text.js` builds the description and the instructions through that function. ... The finder scanned every markdown file under `curriculum`, including the i18n submodule, for event-handler attributes on HTML elements outside code blocks and interactive-editor blocks. ... Reviewers must reject raw HTML with event handlers, `<script>` tags or `javascript:` URLs in curriculum markdown until the parser enforces this. ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# [A05 Injection] XSS via unsanitized curriculum markdown rendering\n\nModerate\n\nraisedadead published GHSA-c7cm-vx87-g6xj Oct 8, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nchallenge-parser (tools)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n< 9ed2bf2c-20261008-1853\n\n## Patched versions\n\n9ed2bf2c-20261008-1853\n\nclient (web)\n\n< 9ed2bf2c-20261008-1853\n\n9ed2bf2c-20261008-1853\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nThe challenge parser turns curriculum markdown into HTML without sanitising it, and the client renders that HTML with `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`. Raw HTML in a curriculum file, including inline event handlers, reaches every learner's browser unchanged. A contributor who gets such HTML merged runs script in every learner's session on that page.\n\n### Details\n\nCurriculum markdown may contain raw HTML. `<img>` tags are common. The parser converts the markdown tree to HTML with raw HTML allowed and no sanitiser:\n    \n    // tools/challenge-parser/parser/plugins/utils/mdast-to-html.js\n    return hastToHTML(\n      mdastToHast(root(nodes), { allowDangerousHtml: true, ...hastOptions }),\n      { allowDangerousHtml: true }\n    );\n\n`add-text.js` builds the description and the instructions through that function. The client renders the result as-is:\n    \n    // client/src/templates/Challenges/components/prism-formatted.tsx\n    <ElementName\n      className={className}\n      dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: text }}\n\n`challenge-description.tsx` passes both the description and the instructions through that component.\n\nExploitation needs a merged pull request. Review is the only control. Localised curriculum files are edited by hand and get less review. Translation contributions are not accepted at present.\n\n### PoC\n\nExecuted locally by the finder.\n\n  1. At commit `bcd2740f`, add `onclick=\"alert('XSS')\"` to the `<img>` tag on line 18 of `curriculum/challenges/english/blocks/lecture-user-interface-design-fundamentals/672baacb7f2f446728e77efe.md`.\n  2. Run the app and open `http://localhost:8000/learn/full-stack-developer/lecture-user-interface-design-fundamentals/what-is-the-importance-of-whitespace-in-design`.\n  3. Click the image. The alert fires.\nScreen recording Screen.Recording.2025-10-30.at.06.55.11.mov\n\n### Impact\n\nScript in a merged curriculum file runs in the freeCodeCamp origin for every learner who opens that page. The attacker needs a merged pull request, so a review mistake is the precondition.\n\n`CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N` - 5.4, Moderate.\n\n### Mitigating factors\n\nThe finder scanned every markdown file under `curriculum`, including the i18n submodule, for event-handler attributes on HTML elements outside code blocks and interactive-editor blocks. No unsafe HTML was found.\n\nAudit script\n    \n    const fs = require('fs');\n    const path = require('path');\n    \n    // Regex for event handler attributes on HTML elements\n    const EVENT_ATTR_REGEX =\n      /<(i m g|s v g|i f r a m e|o b j e c t|e m b e d|a|b u t t o n|i n p u t|f o r m|v i d e o|a u d i o)[^>]*\\s(o n\\w+)\\s*=/gi;\n    // Regex for code blocks (triple backticks)\n    const CODE_BLOCK_REGEX = /```[\\s\\S]*?```/g;\n    // Regex for :::interactive_editor blocks\n    const INTERACTIVE_EDITOR_REGEX = /:::i n t e r a c t i v e_e d i t o r[\\s\\S]*?:::/g;\n    \n    function scanFile(filePath) {\n      const content = fs.readFileSync(filePath, 'utf8');\n      // Remove code blocks and interactive_editor blocks\n      let sanitized = content.replace(CODE_BLOCK_REGEX, '');\n      sanitized = sanitized.replace(INTERACTIVE_EDITOR_REGEX, '');\n      // Find matches\n      const matches = sanitized.match(EVENT_ATTR_REGEX);\n      if (matches) {\n        console.log(`Potential XSS in ${filePath}:`);\n        matches.forEach(m => console.log(`  ${m}`));\n      }\n    }\n    \n    function scanDir(dir) {\n      fs.readdirSync(dir).forEach(file => {\n        const fullPath = path.join(dir, file);\n        if (fs.statSync(fullPath).isDirectory()) {\n          scanDir(fullPath);\n        } else if (file.endsWith('.md')) {\n          scanFile(fullPath);\n        }\n      });\n    }\n    \n    // Usage: node find-xss-in-curriculum.js\n    scanDir('curriculum');\n\n### Patches\n\nPending.\n\n### Workarounds\n\nNone for learners. Reviewers must reject raw HTML with event handlers, `<script>` tags or `javascript:` URLs in curriculum markdown until the parser enforces this.\n\n### Fix direction\n\nValidate the HTML tree in the challenge parser at build time against an allowlist of tags and attributes, and fail the curriculum build on anything else. A build failure is visible to the reviewer. Sanitising the output was tried first and set aside: `sanitize-html` removes suspicious content but cannot report it, so a reviewer would not see what was dropped.\n\n### Severity\n\nModerate\n\n5.4\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n/ 10\n\n#### CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector\n\nNetwork\n\nAttack complexity\n\nLow\n\nPrivileges required\n\nLow\n\nUser interaction\n\nRequired\n\nScope\n\nChanged\n\nConfidentiality\n\nLow\n\nIntegrity\n\nLow\n\nAvailability\n\nNone\n\nLearn more about base metrics\n\n# CVSS v3 base metrics\n\nAttack vector: More severe the more the remote (logically and physically) an attacker can be in order to exploit the vulnerability.\n\nAttack complexity: More severe for the least complex attacks.\n\nPrivileges required: More severe if no privileges are required.\n\nUser interaction: More severe when no user interaction is required.\n\nScope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\nConfidentiality: More severe when loss of data confidentiality is highest, measuring the level of data access available to an unauthorized user.\n\nIntegrity: More severe when loss of data integrity is the highest, measuring the consequence of data modification possible by an unauthorized user.\n\nAvailability: More severe when the loss of impacted component availability is highest.\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nWeakness CWE-79\n\n#### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\nThe product does not neutralize or incorrectly neutralizes user-controllable input before it is placed in output that is used as a web page that is served to other users. Learn more on MITRE.\n\n### Credits\n\n  * huyenltnguyen Reporter\n  * ojeytonwilliams Remediation developer\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via Markdown hyperlink · Advisory · siyuan-note/siyuan · GitHub (https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan/security/advisories/GHSA-rw25-98wq-76qv)\n【turn804800search2】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; https://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan (Go) ... A Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the Markdown rendering engine. An attacker can inject malicious JavaScript into a Markdown text/note. ... https://book.hacktricks.wiki/en/pentesting-web/xss-cross-site-scripting/xss-in-markdown.html ... Scope: More severe when a scope change occurs, e.g. one vulnerable component impacts resources in components beyond its security scope.\n\n# Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via Markdown hyperlink\n\nModerate\n\n88250 published GHSA-rw25-98wq-76qv Feb 5, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nhttps://github.com/siyuan-note/siyuan (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\nv3.5.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\nv3.5.5\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nA Stored Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability exists in the Markdown rendering engine. An attacker can inject malicious JavaScript into a Markdown text/note. When another user clicks the rendered content, the script executes in the context of their session.\n\n### Details\n\nVulnerability Type: Stored XSS\nAffected Component: Markdown Parser / Frontend Renderer\nAttack Vector: Maliciously crafted Markdown/HTML tags that bypass sanitization.\nThe issue stems from insufficient filtering of specific HTML attributes or event handlers within the Markdown conversion process.\n\n### PoC\n\n  1. Write a text in the page\n  2. Paste the payloads:\n`[a](javascript:prompt(document.domain))`\nor\n`[Click` Me](javascript:alert('XSS'))\n\nReferences: (Many working payloads)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\njusthtml: to_markdown() code-span blank-line breakout enables XSS · GHSA-jf6w-2mvx-633j · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-jf6w-2mvx-633j)\n【turn804800search3】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: last month; Moderate severity GitHub Reviewed Published May 22, 2026 in EmilStenstrom/justhtml • Updated Jun 25, 2026 ... A blank line (`\\n\\n`) in that text terminates the inline span in any compliant Markdown renderer, so attacker-controlled text that survived HTML sanitization is emitted unescaped after the blank line and is re-parsed as live raw HTML/Markdown — yielding XSS in the default configuration. ... `to_markdown()` is documented as a safety surface. `docs/text.md` states the guarantee applies \"to the HTML produced by rendering that Markdown with a compliant Markdown renderer,\" and `SECURITY.md` promises `to_markdown()` \"escapes line-start Markdown markers that could change block structure\" and \"uses code fences long enough to contain backticks safely.\" ... CommonMark render : '<p><code>q&lt;img src=x onerror=__POC_XSS_MARKER__()&gt;</code></p>' ... Scope is Changed: the injected script runs in the origin of the page that renders the Markdown, a different security authority than the library that produced it. ...   * Novelty: same vulnerability class as two prior, already-fixed `to_markdown()` advisories but a distinct, still-unfixed variant.\n\n## justhtml: to_markdown() code-span blank-line breakout enables XSS\n\nModerate severity GitHub Reviewed Published May 22, 2026 in EmilStenstrom/justhtml • Updated Jun 25, 2026\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\njusthtml (pip)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 0.9.0, <= 1.21.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\n1.22.0\n\n## Description\n\n# justhtml: to_markdown() code-span blank-line breakout enables XSS\n\n### Summary\n\nIn `justhtml` 0.9.0 through 1.21.0, `to_markdown()` renders `<code>` text (and `<pre>` text inside a link) as an inline Markdown code span whose only protection is backtick-fence length. A blank line (`\\n\\n`) in that text terminates the inline span in any compliant Markdown renderer, so attacker-controlled text that survived HTML sanitization is emitted unescaped after the blank line and is re-parsed as live raw HTML/Markdown — yielding XSS in the default configuration. Likely CWE-79 (Cross-site Scripting) arising from CWE-116 (Improper Encoding/Escaping of Output).\n\n### Details\n\n`to_markdown()` is documented as a safety surface. `docs/text.md` states the guarantee applies \"to the HTML produced by rendering that Markdown with a compliant Markdown renderer,\" and `SECURITY.md` promises `to_markdown()` \"escapes line-start Markdown markers that could change block structure\" and \"uses code fences long enough to contain backticks safely.\"\n\nThe inline code-span helper only sizes the backtick fence; it never accounts for block boundaries:\n\n`src/justhtml/node.py:32-41` (tag `v1.21.0`):\n    \n    def _markdown_code_span(s: str | None) -> str:\n        if s is None:\n            s = \"\"\n        # Use a backtick fence longer than any run of backticks inside.\n        fence = _markdown_backtick_fence(s, minimum=1)\n        # CommonMark requires a space if the content starts/ends with backticks.\n        needs_space = s.startswith(\"`\") or s.endswith(\"`\")\n        if needs_space:\n            return f\"{fence} {s} {fence}\"\n        return f\"{fence}{s}{fence}\"\n\nThe element's text is taken verbatim (`strip=False`, so embedded newlines are preserved) and routed into that helper:\n### Impact\n\nThis is a cross-site scripting vulnerability (CWE-79). It affects any application that follows the documented pipeline: sanitize untrusted HTML with `JustHTML(...)` under default settings, call `to_markdown()`, and render the result with a CommonMark-compliant renderer (raw-HTML passthrough is the CommonMark default).\n\nAn attacker only needs to control HTML text inside a `<code>` element, or a `<pre>` element within a link — no custom policy and no `sanitize=False`. Any user who then views the rendered page executes attacker-controlled script in their own origin, enabling cookie/session theft or actions performed as the victim.\n\nSeverity: CVSS 3.1 6.1 (Moderate),\n`CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:R/S:C/C:L/I:L/A:N`. Scope is Changed: the injected script runs in the origin of the page that renders the Markdown, a different security authority than the library that produced it.\n\n### Recommended fix\n\nDo not represent text containing a block boundary as an inline code span. In `_markdown_code_span` / the `<code>` and in-link `<pre>` dispatch (`src/justhtml/node.py:1061-1078`), if the content contains a blank line (or any `\\n`), emit it as a fenced code block — reusing the existing block path at lines 1066-1074, whose fence is not broken by blank lines — or collapse newlines in inline-code content. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nmo has a XSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering · GHSA-vccx-p757-pv6h · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-vccx-p757-pv6h)\n【turn804800search4】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; ## mo has a XSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering\n\nLow severity GitHub Reviewed Published Mar 18, 2026 in k1LoW/mo ... When rendering Markdown files containing inline SVG elements with `<script>` tags, the embedded JavaScript is executed in the browser. ... These differ from security-enhancing techniques/technologies (ref Attack Complexity) as the primary purpose of these conditions is not to explicitly mitigate attacks, but rather, emerge naturally as a consequence of the deployment and execution of the vulnerable system.\n\n## mo has a XSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering\n\nLow severity GitHub Reviewed Published Mar 18, 2026 in k1LoW/mo\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/k1LoW/mo (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 0.18.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\n0.18.5\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nWhen rendering Markdown files containing inline SVG elements with `<script>` tags, the embedded JavaScript is executed in the browser. This is due to `rehype-raw` passing raw HTML (including SVG) through to the DOM without sanitization.\n\n### PoC\n    \n    <svg><script>alert(1)</script></svg>\n\nEmbedding the above in a Markdown file opened with mo causes the script to execute.\n\n### Impact\n\nArbitrary JavaScript execution in the context of the mo application when a user opens a malicious Markdown file. This could lead to data exfiltration of other opened Markdown file contents.\n\n### Remediation\n\nFixed by adding `rehype-sanitize` to the Markdown rendering pipeline, which strips dangerous elements such as `<script>` while preserving safe HTML.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfilebrowser allows Stored Cross-Site Scripting through the Markdown preview function · CVE-2025-52902 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-4wx8-5gm2-2j97)\n【turn804800search5】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; Any JavaScript code that is part of a Markdown file uploaded by a user will be executed by the browser ... The most thorough fix would be to reconfigure the application's Markdown parser to ignore all HTML elements and only render rich text which is part of the Markdown specification. If HTML rendering is considered to be a required feature, an HTML sanitizer like DOMPurify should be used, preferably in conjunction with a Content Security Policy (CSP). ...   * https://github.com/sbaresearch/advisories/tree/public/2025/SBA-ADV-20250325-04_Filebrowser_Stored_XSS\n\n## filebrowser allows Stored Cross-Site Scripting through the Markdown preview function\n\nHigh severity GitHub Reviewed Published Jun 26, 2025 in filebrowser/filebrowser • Updated Aug 4, 2025\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/filebrowser/filebrowser (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 1.11.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\nNone\n\ngithub.com/filebrowser/filebrowser/v2 (Go)\n\n< 2.33.7\n\n2.33.7\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Markdown preview function of File Browser v2.32.0 is vulnerable to Stored Cross-Site-Scripting (XSS). Any JavaScript code that is part of a Markdown file uploaded by a user will be executed by the browser\n\n## Impact\n\nA user can upload a malicious Markdown file to the application which can contain arbitrary HTML code. If another user within the same scope clicks on that file, a rendered preview is opened. JavaScript code that has been included will be executed.\n\nMalicious actions that are possible include:\n\n  * Obtaining a user's session token\n  * Elevating the attacker's privileges, if the victim is an administrator (e.g., gaining command execution rights)\n\n## Vulnerability Description\n\nMost Markdown parsers accept arbitrary HTML in a document and try rendering it accordingly. For instance, if one creates a file called `xss.md` with the following content:\n    \n    # Hallo\n    \n    <b>foo</b>\n    \n    <img src=\"xx\" onerror=alert(9)>\n    <i>bar</i>\n\nBold and italic text will be rendered. Also, the renderer used in File Browser will try to display the image and execute the code in the `onerror` event handler.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n\nThe screenshot shows that the code from the file mentioned above has actually been executed in the victim's browser:\n\n## Recommended Countermeasures\n\nThe most thorough fix would be to reconfigure the application's Markdown parser to ignore all HTML elements and only render rich text which is part of the Markdown specification. If HTML rendering is considered to be a required feature, an HTML sanitizer like DOMPurify should be used, preferably in conjunction with a Content Security Policy (CSP).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNiceGUI's XSS vulnerability in ui.markdown() allows arbitrary JavaScript execution through unsanitized HTML content · CVE-2026-25516 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-v82v-c5x8-w282)\n【turn804800search6】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last week; ## NiceGUI's XSS vulnerability in ui.markdown() allows arbitrary JavaScript execution through unsanitized HTML content\n\nModerate severity GitHub Reviewed Published Feb 5, 2026 in zauberzeug/nicegui • Updated Feb 7, 2026 ... Unlike other NiceGUI components that render HTML (`ui.html()`, `ui.chat_message()`, `ui.interactive_image()`), the `ui.markdown()` component does not provide or require a `sanitize` parameter, leaving applications vulnerable to XSS attacks. ... Add a `sanitize` parameter to `ui.markdown()` consistent with other HTML-rendering components, and/or add an `escape_html` parameter.\n\n## NiceGUI's XSS vulnerability in ui.markdown() allows arbitrary JavaScript execution through unsanitized HTML content\n\nModerate severity GitHub Reviewed Published Feb 5, 2026 in zauberzeug/nicegui • Updated Feb 7, 2026\n\n## Package\n\nnicegui (pip)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= 3.6.1\n\n## Patched versions\n\n3.7.0\n\n## Description\n\n## Description\n\nThe `ui.markdown()` component uses the `markdown2` library to convert markdown content to HTML, which is then rendered via `innerHTML`. By default, `markdown2` allows raw HTML to pass through unchanged. This means that if an application renders user-controlled content through `ui.markdown()`, an attacker can inject malicious HTML containing JavaScript event handlers.\n\nUnlike other NiceGUI components that render HTML (`ui.html()`, `ui.chat_message()`, `ui.interactive_image()`), the `ui.markdown()` component does not provide or require a `sanitize` parameter, leaving applications vulnerable to XSS attacks.\n\n## Proof of Concept\n    \n    from nicegui import ui\n    \n    # User-controlled input containing malicious payload\n    user_input = 'Hello! <img src=x onerror=\"alert(\\'XSS\\')\">'\n    \n    ui.markdown(user_input)  # XSS executes when page loads\n    \n    ui.run()\n\nWhen this page loads, the JavaScript in the `onerror` handler executes, potentially allowing an attacker to:\n\n  * Steal session cookies or authentication tokens\n  * Perform actions on behalf of the user\n  * Redirect users to malicious sites\n  * Modify page content\n\n## Impact\n\nApplications that render user-provided content through `ui.markdown()` are vulnerable to stored or reflected XSS attacks. This is particularly concerning for:\n\n  * Chat applications displaying user messages\n  * CMS or documentation systems with user-editable content\n  * Any application that displays markdown from untrusted sources\n\n## Remediation\n\nA release has been published in version 3.7.0.\n\n### For Users (Immediate Workaround)\n\nUntil a fix is released, do not pass untrusted content directly to `ui.markdown()`. Instead, use one of these approaches:\n\nOption 1: Convert and sanitize manually using `ui.html()`\n    \n    import markdown2\n    from html_sanitizer import Sanitizer\n    \n    sanitizer = Sanitizer()\n    \n    def safe_markdown(content: str) -> None:\n        \"\"\"Render markdown with HTML sanitization.\"\"\"\n        html = markdown2.markdown(content)\n        ui.html(sanitizer.sanitize(html), sanitize=False)\n    \n    # Usage\n    safe_markdown(user_input)\n\nOption 2: Escape HTML before markdown conversion (if raw HTML not needed)\n    \n    import html\n    \n    # Escape HTML entities - prevents any HTML from being interpreted\n    ui.markdown(html.escape(user_input))\n\n### Proposed Fix\n\nAdd a `sanitize` parameter to `ui.markdown()` consistent with other HTML-rendering components, and/or add an `escape_html` parameter.\n\n### References\n\n  * GHSA-v82v-c5x8-w282\n  * zauberzeug/nicegui@`f1f7533`\n  * https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-25516\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nn8n Has Stored Cross-site Scripting via Markdown Rendering in Workflow UI · CVE-2026-25054 · GitHub Advisory Database · GitHub (https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-qpq4-pw7f-pp8w)\n【turn804800search7】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; High severity GitHub Reviewed Published Feb 4, 2026 in n8n-io/n8n • Updated Feb 4, 2026 ... A Cross-site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability existed in a markdown rendering component used in n8n's interface, including workflow sticky notes and other areas that support markdown content. ... n8n has adopted CVSS 4.0 as primary score for all security advisories.\n\n## n8n Has Stored Cross-site Scripting via Markdown Rendering in Workflow UI\n\nHigh severity GitHub Reviewed Published Feb 4, 2026 in n8n-io/n8n • Updated Feb 4, 2026\n\nVulnerability details Dependabot alerts 0\n\n## Package\n\nn8n (npm)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n>= 2.0.0, < 2.2.1\n\n< 1.123.9\n\n## Patched versions\n\n2.2.1\n\n1.123.9\n\n## Description\n\n## Impact\n\nA Cross-site Scripting (XSS) vulnerability existed in a markdown rendering component used in n8n's interface, including workflow sticky notes and other areas that support markdown content.\n\nAn authenticated user with permission to create or modify workflows could abuse this to execute scripts with same-origin privileges when other users interact with a maliciously crafted workflow. This could lead to session hijacking and account takeover.\n\n## Patches\n\nThe issue has been fixed in n8n versions 2.2.1 and 1.123.9. Users should upgrade to these versions or later to remediate the vulnerability.\n\n## Workarounds\n\nIf upgrading is not immediately possible, administrators should consider the following temporary mitigations:\n\n  * Limit workflow creation and editing permissions to fully trusted users only.\n  * Review existing workflows for potentially malicious markdown content in sticky notes and other components.\n  * Educate users about the risks of opening workflows from untrusted sources.\n\nn8n has adopted CVSS 4.0 as primary score for all security advisories. CVSS 3.1 vector strings are provided for backward compatibility.\n\nCVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:R/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:N\n\n### References\n\n  * GHSA-qpq4-pw7f-pp8w\n  * https://nvd.nist.gov/vuln/detail/CVE-2026-25054\n\ncsuermann published to n8n-io/n8n Feb 4, 2026\n\nPublished by the National Vulnerability Database Feb 4, 2026\n\nPublished to the GitHub Advisory Database Feb 4, 2026\n\nReviewed Feb 4, 2026\n\nLast updated Feb 4, 2026\n\n### Severity\n\nHigh\n\n8.5\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n/ 10\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nXSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering · Advisory · k1LoW/mo · GitHub (https://github.com/k1LoW/mo/security/advisories/GHSA-vccx-p757-pv6h)\n【turn804800search8】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # XSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering ... github.com/k1LoW/mo (Go) ... When rendering Markdown files containing inline SVG elements with `<script>` tags, the embedded JavaScript is executed in the browser.\n\nk1LoW / mo Public\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 57\n\n# XSS via inline SVG script tags in Markdown rendering\n\nLow\n\nk1LoW published GHSA-vccx-p757-pv6h Mar 18, 2026\n\n## Package\n\ngithub.com/k1LoW/mo (Go)\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<= v0.18.4\n\n## Patched versions\n\nv0.18.5\n\n## Description\n\n### Summary\n\nWhen rendering Markdown files containing inline SVG elements with `<script>` tags, the embedded JavaScript is executed in the browser. This is due to `rehype-raw` passing raw HTML (including SVG) through to the DOM without sanitization.\n\n### PoC\n    \n    <svg><script>alert(1)</script></svg>\n\nEmbedding the above in a Markdown file opened with mo causes the script to execute.\n\n### Impact\n\nArbitrary JavaScript execution in the context of the mo application when a user opens a malicious Markdown file. This could lead to data exfiltration of other opened Markdown file contents.\n\n### Remediation\n\nFixed by adding `rehype-sanitize` to the Markdown rendering pipeline, which strips dangerous elements such as `<script>` while preserving safe HTML.\n\n### Severity\n\n# CVSS overall score\n\nThis score calculates overall vulnerability severity from 0 to 10 and is based on the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS).\n\n/ 10\n\n#### CVSS v4 base metrics\n\n##### Exploitability Metrics\n\nAttack Vector Network\n\nAttack Complexity High\n\nAttack Requirements None\n\nPrivileges Required None\n\nUser interaction Passive\n\n##### Vulnerable System Impact Metrics\n\nConfidentiality Low\n\nIntegrity None\n\nAvailability None\n\n##### Subsequent System Impact Metrics\n\nConfidentiality None\n\nIntegrity None\n\nAvailability None\n\nCVSS:4.0/AV:N/AC:H/AT:N/PR:N/UI:P/VC:L/VI:N/VA:N/SC:N/SI:N/SA:N\n\n### CVE ID\n\nNo known CVE\n\n### Weaknesses\n\nNo CWEs\n\n### Credits\n\n  * yagihash Reporter\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass · Advisory · Budibase/budibase · GitHub (https://github.com/Budibase/budibase/security/advisories/GHSA-57p7-9h9w-xqpw)\n【turn804800search9】 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 4 days ago; # Stored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass ... The published-app Text component (`packages/client/src/components/app/Text.svelte:57`) binds `text` to any builder-supplied expression, typically `{{ Repeater.<column> }}`, and forwards to `MarkdownViewer`. ... This is the canonical Budibase pattern for rendering user-submitted rich text. ... CloudFront serves `*.mjs` with `Content-Type: text/javascript`, so the browser's strict MIME check on `<script src>` passes without any upload-time MIME tampering. ... Forces browsers to download rather than render attachments, which removes the direct-navigation XSS path on self-hosted. ... #### Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')\n\n# Stored XSS in Text component: BASIC users execute JS in admin session via MarkdownViewer innerHTML + CDN+srcdoc CSP bypass\n\nHigh\n\nmjashanks published GHSA-57p7-9h9w-xqpw May 21, 2026\n\n## Software\n\nBudibase/budibase\n\n## Affected versions\n\n<3.39.0\n\n## Patched versions\n\nNone\n\n## Description\n\n## Summary\n\nThe Budibase Text component renders markdown by assigning `marked.parse(markdown)` straight to `innerHTML` with no sanitizer (`packages/bbui/src/Markdown/MarkdownViewer.svelte:22`). Any column a builder binds to a Text component in Markdown mode is a stored-XSS sink writable by every BASIC app user with WRITE on the underlying table. Bob, an App User that Alice invited as BASIC, submits a row whose body contains an `<iframe srcdoc>` that loads a script from `d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net`, the CloudFront distribution that serves both Budibase's own runtime and user-uploaded attachments. That origin is on the app's CSP `script-src` allowlist. The srcdoc document inherits the parent CSP and is same-origin with the Budibase app, so Bob's script runs as Alice the moment she opens the screen. On Cloud this is a free-tier invite accepting any email.\n  * Alice's app has a table with a long-form column (`body`) and a screen that renders `body` through a Text component in Markdown mode. This is the canonical Budibase pattern for rendering user-submitted rich text.\n\n### Variant A: Cloud, strict CSP (verified on iwnyub5k.budibase.app)\n\nBudibase Cloud ships a strict CSP:\n    \n    `script-src 'self' 'unsafe-eval' https://*.budibase.net https://cdn.budi.live\n               https://d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net ... 'nonce-<per-request>';\n    frame-src 'self' https:;\n    `\n\nNo `'unsafe-inline'`. Inline event handlers and `javascript:` URIs are blocked. The escape route is two design choices that combine into a bypass:\n\n  1. The CloudFront distribution that serves Budibase's own static JS is on the `script-src` allowlist. That is `d2l5prqdbvm3op.cloudfront.net`. Any script served from there is CSP-trusted. The CSP also lists `cdn.budi.live`, which does not currently resolve to an IP, so only the cloudfront.net hostname is load-bearing.\n"}
